  "sport": "basketball",
  "title": "Attack, retreat, change direction",
  "description": "Place a cone on each side about 6–7 metres from the basket, level with the three-point line. Players line up in two lines at half-court, ideally with a ball each. The first player takes two hard dribbles straight at the cone as if it were a defender, retreats with two protected dribbles and makes a change of direction (crossover, inside-out or between the legs). Then one single long dribble straight at the basket into a lay-up or a pull-up jump shot. Get the rebound and join the back of the other line.\n\nThe lines alternate so there are never two players at the basket at once. Vary the change of direction every round and work both hands equally.\n\nProgression: raise the speed until the ball is sometimes lost – that is fine while learning something new. Replace the cone with a passive defender who backs off when the attacker attacks. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Eyes up all the time – see the basket, not the ball."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "The attack dribbles must be hard so the defence is forced to back up."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Retreat with your body between the ball and the defender, low with bent knees."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Push the ball far out in front – one dribble should take you all the way to the finish."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
